# Who is asked, before anything is automated.
#
# The order here is not an accident of taste. sdd107 puts ownership FIRST -- before
# hierarchy, before any drift detector -- because a detector that fires with nobody
# to answer it produces alerts with no addressee, and a team learns to scroll past
# those within a week. This file is the addressee.
#
# GitHub applies the LAST matching rule, so the specific paths below deliberately
# carry no catch-all above them: a change to `app/` needs no owner's attention,
# a change to what `app/` is judged AGAINST does.
#
# Two properties worth knowing before relying on it:
#   - A CODEOWNERS file above 3 MB stops working SILENTLY -- no owner is summoned
#     and nobody gets an error. This one is a few hundred bytes.
#   - Owners are auto-requested on every pull request regardless of branch rules.
#     Only "Require review from Code Owners" in the ruleset turns the request into
#     a condition. Until a second reviewer exists, that rule would lock the sole
#     author out of their own pull request -- see the plan's Phase 0 note. The file
#     is useful either way; the ruleset decides whether it BLOCKS or merely ASKS.
#
# The organisation has no teams yet. A team is the better owner than a person --
# a person leaves and the rule silently points at nobody -- so replace @jarekwas
# with @Scalo-Sales-Engineering-Consulting/<team> once one exists.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE THREE QUESTIONS, ANSWERED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sdd107 asks three things of every layer that has an owner, and a blank answer
# to any of them marks the layer ORPHANED. Two of them GitHub answers by itself;
# the third it cannot, so it is answered here or nowhere.
#
#   1. Who may change it?      Anyone. This is not a permission system, and
#                              nothing below narrows write access.
#   2. Who must approve it?    The owner on the matching line, IF AND ONLY IF
#                              the branch ruleset turns on "Require review from
#                              Code Owners". Without that setting these lines
#                              request a review and do not require one.
#   3. Who gets the drift      The same owner. There is no separate alert
#      alert?                  routing and deliberately so: sdd107's own advice
#                              is ownership first, automation second, and a
#                              drift router built before a second owner exists
#                              would deliver every alert to the person who
#                              wrote the rule that fired it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUCCESSION -- what happens when an owner goes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Succession is part of the topology, not an afterthought to it: an owner who
# leaves without one turns every line pointing at them into a rule with no
# addressee, and nothing in GitHub reports that. The rule here has to work while
# the answer is one person, so it is a rule about the FILE rather than about a
# roster:
#
#   * An owner leaving names their successor IN THIS FILE, in the same pull
#     request that removes their access. Not afterwards: afterwards is the
#     window in which the lines point at nobody.
#   * Where no successor is named, the successor is the repository
#     administrator, by default and by omission -- so a line never resolves to
#     nobody, it resolves to the person who has to notice.
#   * A line whose owner no longer has access is a DEFECT and is fixed by
#     editing this file, never by deleting the line. Deleting it removes the
#     rule together with the gap, and the gap is the information.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PER-CONTEXT OWNERSHIP -- the pattern the second context uses
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A bounded context is the natural unit of ownership: it has its own language,
# its own rules and its own screens, so it maps onto a person or a team without
# cutting anything in half. What makes that expressible is a PATH that holds one
# context and nothing else, and as of 2026-09-07 every tree has one. The same
# word names the context in all five, which is why there is no mapping file to
# keep current:
#
#   /spec/contexts/<name>.md              the rules
#   /contracts/openapi/<name>.yaml        the frozen boundary
#   /app/contexts/<name>/                 the backend, whole
#   /frontend/src/contexts/<name>/        the screen, whole
#   /e2e/suite/features/<name>.feature    what proves it
#
# With one context, per-context rows would be noise: each would name the same
# person the catch-all rules above already name, and a file of rows that all say
# the same thing is a file people stop reading. The pattern, for the second:
#
#   /spec/contexts/billing.md            @Scalo-Sales-Engineering-Consulting/billing
#   /contracts/openapi/billing.yaml      @Scalo-Sales-Engineering-Consulting/billing
#   /app/contexts/billing/               @Scalo-Sales-Engineering-Consulting/billing
#   /frontend/src/contexts/billing/      @Scalo-Sales-Engineering-Consulting/billing
#   /e2e/suite/features/billing.feature  @Scalo-Sales-Engineering-Consulting/billing
#
# Five lines, no overlap with any other context, and nothing above them to
# override -- which is the whole reason the trees were cut this way.

# The specification: what the system is supposed to do. Every change is judged
# against these documents, so a change TO them is a change to the yardstick.
/spec/                          @jarekwas

# The boundary contracts. Hand-written and versioned by decision (constitution,
# art. VI): the contract is the authority and the code is validated against it.
/contracts/                     @jarekwas

# The schema's only owner. `alembic/versions/` IS the database contract -- there is
# no contracts/ directory for it, and that is recorded as a decision rather than a
# gap in contracts/README.md. A revision is as irreversible as this repo gets.
/alembic/versions/              @jarekwas

# The change process itself -- the code that decides whether a change may merge --
# whole: the skills, the engine, its commands under bin/, the hooks and the MCP servers.
/.claude/                       @jarekwas
/.specconf/                     @jarekwas

# What may deploy, and what decides whether a merge is allowed. A pull request
# that edits the gates is a pull request that edits its own judge.
/.github/                       @jarekwas
/infra/                         @jarekwas
/scripts/deploy.sh              @jarekwas
/scripts/release.sh             @jarekwas
